00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:02.389 align:start position:0%
 
hello and welcome you're watching the  daily climate show coming up  the uk's emissions targets net zero by  2050 won't be reached with current  policies according to a new report  the eu agrees new cars must be emissions  free after 2035  and nato announces its first ever  climate targets aiming for a 45  emissions reduction across the alliance  hello and welcome to the daily climate  show where we track the changes  happening to our planet hear from those  hit hardest by those changes and reports  on the race for solutions now the uk is  not delivering on its climate goals  that's the conclusion drawn from a new  report released today by the climate  change committee the government's  independent advisors they found major  failures in the delivery of programmes  working to achieve the uk's goal of  becoming net zero by 2050. now last year  the uk's greenhouse gas emissions were  47 below what they were in 1990 that is  one of the fastest declines among  industrialized nations  but plans for cutting emissions will  need to be strengthened for the uk's  climate targets to be met according to  the committee now this is the  government's current pathway for  reducing emissions up to the year 2037.  credible plans exist for 39 of the  emission cuts needed to get there but  there are risks attached to plans for  nearly 60  of the required reductions either  because they're still under development  or because they have no clear timeline  and for five percent of the required  emission reductions plans are either  inadequate or missing altogether now one  of the greatest policy failures the  report found was on installation with  rates of improvement in energy  efficiency continuing to be well below  the necessary level our climate change  in energy correspondent hannah thomas  peter has this report  lord deben is the chairman of the  climate change committee and he's  delivering a grim report on government  plans to get to net zero carbon  emissions by 2050.  his verdict lots of grand promises but  nowhere near enough detail  as things stand with the current program  that the government has  net zero will not happen that is true  quite a number of departments have not  yet got into their stride and what we're  saying is we haven't got any time to  wait you know this is very serious it's  no longer possible to ignore the fact  that we will make this planet  uninhabitable if we don't act now among  the major failures identified the  committee describes a shocking gap in  policy for better insulated homes  richard shears already knows this all  too well he made his house more energy  efficient without any government  guidance and now helps others to do the  same  it can be confusing for people starting  on the retrofit journey because there  are so many different points of contact  and there's so many different messages  out there that  knowing where to start can be can be a  real headache  agriculture and land use are also  singled out for what's described as  glacial progress we really do want to  see that incentivization we want to see  that policy uh really help farmers  formulate a plan but i think we do need  government to take it seriously but it's  not all bad news  the report says that great progress has  been made on electric vehicles and on  renewable energy but it will make for  very uncomfortable reading for the  government  and for boris johnson who as prime  minister has consistently said that  action on climate change is a top  priority  his critics though have never been  convinced  we have a prime minister who essentially  is a climate cheat he's using all the  language to suggest that he's taking  action on climate change but as this  damning report makes so clear he  literally is not doing that there is  this big gulf between his words and his  action  in a statement a government spokesperson  said over the past three decades the uk  has driven down emissions faster than  any other g7 country and we have clear  plans to go further  we are leading the world on climate  change helping over 90 percent of  countries set net zero targets during  our cop 26 presidency up from 30 percent  two years ago  but grabbing the headlines and  delivering on the detail at home are two  very different things and the chasm  between the two must be closed if the  government is to keep its promises on  tackling climate change  hannah thomas peters sky news in  westminster  well joining us now is chaitanya kumar  head of environment and green transition  at the new economics foundation mr kumar  thank you very much for being with us  here on the climate show now it's not a  good look for the government this uh how  can they be made to adopt the  recommendations  i think the the committee's role is to  basically give  a rigorous dispassionate analysis of  where we are and where we are isn't a  good place we obviously made some good  progress in the power sector primarily  but the the thing is a lot of the  changes we've seen in the past sector  are out of sight and therefore quite out  of mind for a lot of people and the  biggest challenge which they've  identified now of course is the heating  and building sector our homes are some  of the leakiest coldest homes in europe  you know and insulating them and  ensuring heat uh that the heat that we  use to warm our homes actually comes  from cleaner sources sort of natural gas  is a big priority so they have got a  very clear set of recommendations in  fact they've got 100 pages of  recommendations in what is one of the  largest longest reports they've produced  and i think the government knows uh at  least in this area that they've got a  lot of work to do and  we just need to see a lot more policy  action in this place  you mentioned that idea of insulation  the government recently said that they  would give up one billion pounds towards  insulating people who um were in  low-income communities for their homes i  mean how far do you think that could go  towards helping  i think it is playing a very important  role so i think i think i'll give the  government credit there but it is a very  small piece of the puzzle uh the  government so the climate change  committee's report basically identifies  that we need about 350 billion pounds of  investment not all public to be clear  it's public money and private capital  that needs to go in but about 350  billion pounds if we need to insulate  and reduce carbon emissions completely  from our housing sector and we're  nowhere near that i think the biggest  gap is the report identifies is what is  called the owner occupied sector that is  homes where  homes that are occupied by the owners  cells that's a big sector more than half  of the country's homes have owners  occupying them and there's absolutely no  policy to actually help incentivize them  to undertake the measures be it  insulating their cavities insulating  their lofts ensuring that they have low  carbon heat in the form of heat pumps  for example there's absolutely zero  policy to actually help them now quasi  quartering the business and energy  minister at a select committee hearing  this morning in fact said that we can  expect some new policy but until the  details are clear  we can safely say and as the company  that we are nowhere close to reaching at  zero in the building sector  and shaytania quickly if you can i  wonder how would the uk be able to  balance you know um reducing fossil fuel  extraction with energy security right  now because it's very important of  course that we have access to our own  energy supplies in light of what's  happening in ukraine  yeah i think i think the greatest  resource we've got is wind  with the blast with abundant sources of  wind energy it's got a bit of solar  we've got geothermal energy and marine  energy there's lots of renewable energy  sources that you can really tap i think  the era of fossil fuels is well behind  us it'll be foolish for any policymaker  any minister to basically come out now  and say  when gas prices are so high that to get  us out of this crisis is to actually  invest more in gas that is absolutely  foolish and we shouldn't be investing  more of our scarce resource  into fossil fuels when we've got cleaner  cheaper and and more renewable sources  that we can export okay che chanyakuma i  appreciate your time today so thank you  so much for being with us  now in other news today in both the  european union and nato have made  significant pledges towards tackling  climate change eu countries reached a  deal backing stricter rules on car  emissions it came after marathon talks  which concluded early this morning the  agreement looks to eliminate carbon  emissions from new cars by 2035. now the  deal was agreed after italy withdrew an  attempt for a five-year delay to the  plans  and nato announced targets to reduce its  emissions of first for the military  alliance the organization described the  climate crisis as the overarching  challenge of our time speaking at the  summit in madrid nato chief yen  stoltenberg said the 30-member alliance  would reduce emissions by at least 45  percent by 2030 and reach net zero  emissions by 2050.  let's get some more of the day's climate  news now first to austria  well this was the scene at last night as  heavy rain and floods hit villages in  the southern state of corinthia one  person is known to have died and one  person is missing authorities have  declared a civil emergency after some  villages in the area were cut off by  mudslides there are warnings that more  rain could be coming  a cocktail of more than 150 pesticides  is harming wildlife in the uk's  countryside a charity has warned the  royal society for the protection of  birds said the combined quantity of  chemicals being used could cover the uk  and its farmland 10 times every year the  rspb warned regulation for pesticides  does not properly assess the real world  impacts of the chemicals being used  and just before we leave we want to know  your climate questions anything that's  ever mystified you or whatever you want  to get to the bottom of if you scan the  qr code on your screen right now you can  send us your questions which will be  answered on our brand new climate show  this weekend hosted by the environmental  broadcaster and author tom heap now it  starts this saturday the 2nd of july at  3 30 pm here on sky news

